  "prompt_text": "This figure illustrates how beneficial soil microbes can improve maize growth through several belowground mechanisms. The plants are shown larger and more vigorous, with an arrow at the side indicating outcomes such as faster growth, enhanced biomass, and heavier grains. Beneath the soil surface, different microbial functions appear to support these gains.\n\nOne microbe-related process converts atmospheric nitrogen into a plant-available form, shown as N₂ becoming NH₄⁺ near the roots. Another highlighted mechanism is phosphate solubilization, where soil phosphorus is made more accessible to the plant. The image also emphasizes facilitated root colonization, suggesting microbes surround or associate closely with roots, likely improving nutrient uptake and root health. A separate callout shows EPS production, linked to soil aggregation and water retention, implying that microbial exudates help create a better soil structure around roots.\n\nTaken together, the figure presents a simple cause-and-effect picture: microbial activity in the rhizosphere improves nutrient availability, root interactions, and soil physical conditions, which in turn supports stronger maize development aboveground. The overall message is that plant-associated microbes can act as natural growth promoters in crop systems.",
